      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "In Utah's intense climate, a standard asphalt shingle roof lasts 15 to 30 years (frequently averaging 20 to 25 years for architectural shingles). High-elevation UV rays, heavy mountain snow loads, and rapid freeze-thaw cycles typically shorten manufacturer estimates. Premium materials like metal or tile last significantly longer, often 40 to 70+ years.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "15 to 30 years"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2,
          3,
          4,
          5
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Lifespan by Roof Material"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "3-Tab Asphalt Shingles: 15 to 20 years"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Architectural Asphalt Shingles:\n20 to 30 years"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Impact-Resistant Shingles (Class 4): 30 to 40 years"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Metal Roofing (Standing Seam):\n40 to 70 years"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Concrete and Clay Tile: 40 to 60 years (though underlayment needs replacing mid-life)"
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          6,
          7
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Why Utah's Climate Ages Roofs Faster"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "High-Elevation UV Rays: Strong mountain sun bakes and dries out asphalt shingles, making them brittle over time."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Freeze-Thaw Cycles:\nMelting snow refreezes in gutters and edges, creating ice dams that lift shingles and force water underneath."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Heavy Snow Loads: Accumulating winter weight stresses the structural decking and strains aging materials."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Poor Attic Ventilation:\nTrapped heat and moisture warp decking and cook shingles from the underside during hot summers."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          8,
          9,
          10
        ]
      },
